<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heinrich von Brühl</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1700">1700</a>–<a href="/years/1763">1763</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Count Heinrich von Brühl was the leading minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-iii">Augustus III</a>, Elector of Saxony and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, from the late 1730s until 1763. He controlled finance and patronage and promoted the arts, but his extravagance left the state heavily in debt, and Saxony was unprepared when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> invaded in 1756. The Brühl Terrace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> bears his name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heinrich-von-bruhl.jpg" alt="Heinrich von Brühl" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marcello Bacciarelli · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marcello_Bacciarelli_-_Heinrich_Graf_von_Br%C3%BChl.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Brühl was born on 13 August 1700 at Gangloffsömmern, the son of Johann Moritz von Brühl, Oberhofmarschall at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxe-weissenfels">Saxe-Weissenfels</a>, who was ruined and had to give up the family estate. After serving as a page to the dowager duchess of Saxe-Weissenfels, he entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> as a Silberpage on 16 April 1719 and won the favour of Elector Frederick Augustus I, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-ii-the-strong">Augustus II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, for whom he raised money. In 1731 he became Chief Receiver of Taxes and Minister of the Interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-ii-the-strong">Augustus II</a> died in 1733, Brühl gained the trust of his heir, Frederick Augustus II, by bringing him the late king's papers and jewels at once, and he raised money for the heir's election as King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-iii">Augustus III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Made a Reichsgraf on 27 November 1737, he was in effect sole minister from 1738, and the title of Prime Minister was created for him in 1746. He controlled all access to the king, took large grants of land and drew the salaries of many offices at once.</p><p>Brühl played a part in the Diplomatic Revolution of 1756 that brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> together, but to meet the king's demands he ran up debts and greatly reduced the army. At the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> army numbered only 17,000 men, and after a few weeks it surrendered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pirna">Pirna</a> for want of supplies and was dissolved. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick II of Prussia</a> mocked him as having more garments, watches, laces, boots and slippers than any man of the age.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Brühl died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> on 28 October 1763, a few weeks after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-iii">Augustus III</a>. The new elector, Frederick Christian, ordered an inquiry into his administration, and his fortune, including palaces at Pförten, Oberlichtenau and Wachau-Seifersdorf, was sequestered but later restored to his family. His spending also served the arts: the Meissen Swan Service of over 2,200 pieces was made for him, the architect Johann Christoph Knöffel began his palace and terrace on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> in 1736, and his picture gallery was bought by Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1768.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Heinrich von Brühl” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…proved costly: the wars of Augustus the Strong, the expense of royal display and the government of Count <b>Heinrich von Brühl</b> under Augustus III left large debts, and the Seven Years’ War brought occupation, heavy requisitions and the destruction of much of Dresden in the Prussian bombardment of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gangloffsömmern, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.19,10.94&amp;year=1740" aria-label="Gangloffsömmern on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4798 390 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Heinrich von Brühl"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5248 660h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5248 660h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.19,10.94&amp;year=1740">What was here in 1740? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
